\f1\fs22 Hayley took the Rail home, and instantly regretted it. At this time of night on a Friday \'85 even the Airborne carriages were invaded by \'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A sou-easter breeze from the Tasman whipped and teased turbid tentacles out from the top of the Layer. The Rail line plunged in and out of fog banks. The aircon even in the airborne upper floors of the carriages couldn\'92t filter all the particulates. Hayley coughed while others in the carriage seemed unaffected as they \'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As much to take her attention off the weather, Hayley browsed through the pictures of Halo she\'92d skimmed off Abe\'92s tablet. They were all hastily taken, the framing was off, and sometimes either Halo\'92s or his target\'92s face was obscured, but there was more than enough in them to establish patterns. Halo possessed the urbane smile of a confidence trickster, of a pick-up player who knew the odds were stacked in his favour. He had the face of a man completely at ease, able to portray his best self, because the outcome was already assured. The woman, however \'85 the women \'85 all leaned in towards Halo while he, at best appeared aloof, if not preparing to back away. The women rested their hand on Halo\'92s chest somewhere between his heart and his shoulder blade, or on his hip. Hayley magnified image after image and saw dilated pupils, daintily chewed bottom lips, hair brushed back behind ears, flushed cheeks. She shifted her focus to Halo. He wore a benign if slightly disinterested smile. But his eyes\'97they were always on the woman, gently beholding her, Hayley thought. The one picture struck her: Halo\'92s eyes were a strange shade off lilac and\'97it may just be a photographic artefact\'97they seemed brighter than his face, as if they glowed, just a little.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley swiped back to the beginning of the album and started going through the pictures again. As she inspected the photos closer, she noted first one, then another in which Halo\'92s eyes were lilac. She found another in which his eyes appeared to be subtly brighter than the rest of the image; then one in which one eye was lilac and the other was \'85 kinda grey. She was swiping around wildly now, sampling random images, concluded Halo\'92s natural eye colour as grey or greenish grey. Now that she looked closer, Hayley noticed quite a variation in Halo\'92s pupil sizes, as if he possessed moment-by-moment control over them.  Even his eyelashes seemed to grow and shrink, though that was probably an illusion borne of his changing eye colour. She returned to the image in which his eyes differed in colour. Perhaps Abe had caught the moment in which the colour was transitioning, and one eye lagged slightly behind the other. Hayley knew what could be causing that, and found the shock of it forcing her gaze off her tablet and towards another commuter who was sitting on the other side of the carriage, passively observing her. \'91Empathy tech,\'92 Hayley told the commuter, though not loud enough for her to hear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley couldn\'92t sleep. She rolled and tossed. She committed pillow abuse and sheet strangulation. She made the ceiling blush from all the staring she gave it. She was so wired, she got up and pleaded with her shower to deliver the most stress-relieving, enervating torrent of hot water it could pump. She pleaded with her towels to be as snuggly as they could as she dried herself and wrapped her hair in one. She just nodded at her gown and slippers; they knew what their missions were.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the kitchen she set coffee brewing, opened her puter on the island counter, drew up a stool and commenced what she was best at: digging up information. She went to an identity site and submitted one of Abe\'92s pictures, and was quickly presented with a profile. A heading that read 
\f2\i Haloren Smythe 
\f1\i0 ran as a lintel atop a summary paragraph in the right column of the page. In the left column, a picture, clearly of Halo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The summary profile listed Halo\'92s occupation as 
\f2\i Life Coach
\f1\i0 . Hayley sneered. All the life coaches she met\'97and she made certain there were not many\'97seemed to be obsessed with coaching the life 
\f2\i out
\f1\i0  of their clients. Hayley knew no one who benefitted from their services, although that could have more to do with the company Hayley kept.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Give me everything on Haloren Smythe,\'92 she ordered her computer which embarked immediately on familiar action. While it chugged, or fizzed, cogitated, coagulated, or whatever puters did these days, Hayley rescued the coffee pot from its steaming squeals, poured herself a cup, sniffed and scrunched her nose. She tentatively sipped. It tasted like piss. She threw it in the sink then boiled the kettle for a cup of tea.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The tea tasted like a different kind of piss. Perhaps with an infection in it. She knew she shouldn\'92t have used that coffee cup they way she did back in the office. It was going to be a long night.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With a glass of synthetic milk flavoured by a glug of tropical rum, she sat to her puter and started browsing, trawling through the data swamps.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Unlike Supaporn\'92 records, there were plenty of Halo\'92s to sift through. A normal amount of health, career and consumer activity. The occasional scraps with local government, speeding and parking tickets, fines for failing to vote, a normal spectrum of businesses and charities including him in marketing campaigns. VIP subscriptions to pubs, clubs and gymnasia. Hayley searched for any that were connected to the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 , found none, then searched through Halo\'92s armpit data \'85 nothing for the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 . Strange, for a regular.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Another thing didn\'92t tally: Halo\'92s residential address. Hayley checked Abe\'92s photos again: they looked like they\'92d all, or nearly all, been taken in the same venue, which must be the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 .
\f2\i  
\f1\i0 Halo\'92s drinking buddies gave Hayley the impression he was a local, but he lived miles away in a much more affluent harbour-side enclave. Hayley was still liking him as the Nightclub Vampire; he frequented a bar in the epicentre of most of the cases.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Well, there were two addresses she\'92d check out in the morning. Having resolved that, Hayley yawned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s aching bones and compressed butt woke her in the cold of the morning. She\'92d somehow slept on the stool, slumped over her puter. The muscles in her arms burned like she\'92d clung to dear life, hanging over a rocky cliff for hours. Her jaw ached and one side of her face was stencilled red and white in the pattern of her puter keyboard.
